To find the “standard deviation” of the sampling distribution of the sample mean (i.e., the standard error) you use the sample standard deviation divided by the square root of the sample size:

\[
SE = \frac{s}{\sqrt{n}}
    = \frac{1.4}{\sqrt{40}}
    = \frac{1.4}{6.3249}
    \approx 0.2213
\]

So the standard error (the standard deviation of the null‑hypothesis sampling distribution) is approximately **0.22**.
